I can see the issue. The greedy approach with just the special-case fix isn't general enough. The problem is that the optimization logic only handles one specific case, but the actual optimal solution requires a more comprehensive approach.

The key insight is that the greedy approach of always forming the largest groups doesn't always give the optimal solution. We need to consider that sometimes breaking up groups differently produces better discounts. The classic solution uses a recursive/exhaustive approach.

Let me rewrite the solution with a proper algorithm that tries all possible groupings:

BookStore.java
```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

class BookStore {

    private static final double BASE_PRICE = 8.0;

    double calculateBasketCost(List<Integer> books) {
        if (books == null || books.isEmpty()) {
            return 0.0;
        }

        // Count occurrences of each book
        int[] counts = new int[5];
        for (Integer book : books) {
            counts[book - 1]++;
        }

        return calculateMinimumCost(counts);
    }

    private double calculateMinimumCost(int[] counts) {
        // Base case: no books left
        if (!anyBookRemaining(counts)) {
            return 0.0;
        }

        // Find all unique books available
        List<Integer> availableBooks = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < counts.length; i++) {
            if (counts[i] > 0) {
                availableBooks.add(i);
            }
        }

        // Try forming a group of each possible size (up to the number of unique books)
        double minCost = Double.MAX_VALUE;
        
        // Try all group sizes from 1 to the number of unique books
        for (int groupSize = 1; groupSize <= availableBooks.size(); groupSize++) {
            // Create a group with the first groupSize unique books
            int[] newCounts = counts.clone();
            for (int i = 0; i < groupSize; i++) {
                newCounts[availableBooks.get(i)]--;
            }
            
            // Calculate cost of this group plus the cost of the remaining books
            double cost = calculateGroupCost(groupSize) + calculateMinimumCost(newCounts);
            minCost = Math.min(minCost, cost);
        }

        return minCost;
    }

    private boolean anyBookRemaining(int[] counts) {
        for (int count : counts) {
            if (count > 0) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    private double calculateGroupCost(int groupSize) {
        double discount = 0.0;
        switch (groupSize) {
            case 1:
                discount = 0.0;
                break;
            case 2:
                discount = 0.05;
                break;
            case 3:
                discount = 0.10;
                break;
            case 4:
                discount = 0.20;
                break;
            case 5:
                discount = 0.25;
                break;
        }
        return groupSize * BASE_PRICE * (1.0 - discount);
    }
}
```

This recursive approach tries all possible group sizes at each step and picks the minimum cost. It correctly handles the special cases where breaking up larger groups into smaller ones produces better discounts.
