Looking at the code, the issue is that the header uses `std::chrono::system_clock::time_point` but doesn't include the `<chrono>` header. The `<chrono>` include is missing from the header file, which would cause the type to be undefined.

gigasecond.h
```cpp
#if !defined(GIGASECOND_H)
#define GIGASECOND_H

#include <chrono>

namespace gigasecond {

// Calculate the date and time one gigasecond after the given time
std::chrono::system_clock::time_point get_after(std::chrono::system_clock::time_point time);

}  // namespace gigasecond

#endif // GIGASECOND_H
```

gigasecond.cpp
```cpp
#include "gigasecond.h"

namespace gigasecond {

// Calculate the date and time one gigasecond after the given time
std::chrono::system_clock::time_point get_after(std::chrono::system_clock::time_point time) {
    return time + std::chrono::seconds(1000000000);
}

}  // namespace gigasecond
```
