I'll implement a CircularBuffer according to the specifications. Here's my plan:

1. Add instance fields for the buffer, size, read/write pointers, and count
2. Initialize the buffer in the constructor
3. Implement read() to return oldest element and advance read pointer
4. Implement write() to add element or throw exception if full
5. Implement overwrite() to add element overwriting oldest if full
6. Implement clear() to reset buffer state

CircularBuffer.java
```java
import java.util.Arrays;

class CircularBuffer<T> {

    private final int size;
    private final Object[] buffer;
    private int readIndex;
    private int writeIndex;
    private int count;

    CircularBuffer(final int size) {
        this.size = size;
        this.buffer = new Object[size];
        this.readIndex = 0;
        this.writeIndex = 0;
        this.count = 0;
    }

    T read() throws BufferIOException {
        if (count == 0) {
            throw new BufferIOException("Circular buffer is empty.");
        }

        @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
        T value = (T) buffer[readIndex];
        readIndex = (readIndex + 1) % size;
        count--;
        return value;
    }

    void write(T data) throws BufferIOException {
        if (count == size) {
            throw new BufferIOException("Circular buffer is full.");
        }

        buffer[writeIndex] = data;
        writeIndex = (writeIndex + 1) % size;
        count++;
    }

    void overwrite(T data) {
        if (count == size) {
            buffer[writeIndex] = data;
            writeIndex = (writeIndex + 1) % size;
            readIndex = (readIndex + 1) % size;
        } else {
            write(data);
        }
    }

    void clear() {
        Arrays.fill(buffer, null);
        readIndex = 0;
        writeIndex = 0;
        count = 0;
    }

}
