I'll implement variable length quantity (VLQ) encoding and decoding.



## Understanding VLQ



**Encoding**: Split each 32-bit number into 7-bit groups, starting from the 
least significant bits. Emit these groups from most significant to least 
significant, with the continuation bit (bit 7) set on all bytes except the last 
one.



**Decoding**: Read bytes, accumulating 7 bits at a time. When a byte has bit 7 
clear, it's the last byte of that number.



## Implementation



lib.rs

```diff
@@ -3,12 +3,70 @@
     IncompleteNumber,
 }
 
 /// Convert a list of numbers to a stream of bytes encoded with variable length
encoding.
 pub fn to_bytes(values: &[u32]) -> Vec<u8> {
-    todo!("Convert the values {values:?} to a list of bytes")
+    let mut result = Vec::new();
+    
+    for &value in values {
+        if value == 0 {
+            result.push(0);
+            continue;
+        }
+        
+        // Extract 7-bit groups, most significant first
+        let mut groups: Vec<u8> = Vec::new();
+        let mut v = value;
+        while v > 0 {
+            let group = (v & 0x7F) as u8;
+            groups.push(group);
+            v >>= 7;
+        }
+        
+        // Reverse to get most significant group first
+        groups.reverse();
+        
+        // All groups except the last get the continuation bit set
+        for (i, group) in groups.iter().enumerate() {
+            if i + 1 < groups.len() {
+                result.push(group | 0x80);
+            } else {
