I'll implement variable length quantity (VLQ) encoding and decoding.

## Understanding VLQ

**Encoding**: Split each 32-bit number into 7-bit groups, starting from the least significant bits. Emit these groups from most significant to least significant, with the continuation bit (bit 7) set on all bytes except the last one.

**Decoding**: Read bytes, accumulating 7 bits at a time. When a byte has bit 7 clear, it's the last byte of that number.

## Implementation

lib.rs
```rust
#[derive(Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum Error {
    IncompleteNumber,
}

/// Convert a list of numbers to a stream of bytes encoded with variable length encoding.
pub fn to_bytes(values: &[u32]) -> Vec<u8> {
    let mut result = Vec::new();
    
    for &value in values {
        if value == 0 {
            result.push(0);
            continue;
        }
        
        // Extract 7-bit groups, most significant first
        let mut groups: Vec<u8> = Vec::new();
        let mut v = value;
        while v > 0 {
            let group = (v & 0x7F) as u8;
            groups.push(group);
            v >>= 7;
        }
        
        // Reverse to get most significant group first
        groups.reverse();
        
        // All groups except the last get the continuation bit set
        for (i, group) in groups.iter().enumerate() {
            if i + 1 < groups.len() {
                result.push(group | 0x80);
            } else {
                result.push(*group);
            }
        }
    }
    
    result
}

/// Given a stream of bytes, extract all numbers which are encoded in there.
pub fn from_bytes(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<Vec<u32>, Error> {
    let mut result = Vec::new();
    let mut accumulator: u32 = 0;
    let mut in_number = false;
    
    for &byte in bytes {
        in_number = true;
        
        // Check if adding these bits would overflow u32
        // We have 7 new bits to add, shifting existing accumulator left by 7
        if accumulator > 0xFFFFFFFF >> 7 {
            return Err(Error::IncompleteNumber);
        }
        
        accumulator = (accumulator << 7) | ((byte & 0x7F) as u32);
        
        // If bit 7 is clear, this is the last byte of the number
        if byte & 0x80 == 0 {
            result.push(accumulator);
            accumulator = 0;
            in_number = false;
        }
    }
    
    // If we ended mid-number, that's an incomplete number
    if in_number {
        return Err(Error::IncompleteNumber);
    }
    
    Ok(result)
}
```

