I'll fix the issue with the "yellow" case. The problem is that "y" at the beginning of a word should be treated as a consonant, but my current code has a special case that's incorrect.

PigLatinTranslator.java
```java
class PigLatinTranslator {
    private static final String VOWELS = "aeiou";

    public String translate(String word) {
        String[] words = word.split(" ");
        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();

        for (int i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
            if (i > 0) {
                result.append(" ");
            }
            result.append(translateWord(words[i]));
        }

        return result.toString();
    }

    private String translateWord(String word) {
        // Rule 1: Word begins with a vowel, or starts with "xr" or "yt"
        if (isVowel(word.charAt(0)) || word.startsWith("xr") || word.startsWith("yt")) {
            return word + "ay";
        }

        // Find the start of the vowel sound
        int index = 0;
        int length = word.length();

        // Find the consonant cluster
        while (index < length) {
            char c = word.charAt(index);

            // Rule 4: "y" after consonants acts as a vowel
            if (index > 0 && c == 'y') {
                break;
            }

            // Rule 3: handle "qu"
            if (c == 'q' && index + 1 < length && word.charAt(index + 1) == 'u') {
                index += 2;
                break;
            }

            if (isVowel(c)) {
                break;
            }

            index++;
        }

        // Move the consonant cluster to the end and add "ay"
        String prefix = word.substring(0, index);
        String rest = word.substring(index);

        return rest + prefix + "ay";
    }

    private boolean isVowel(char c) {
        return VOWELS.indexOf(Character.toLowerCase(c)) != -1;
    }
}
```
