Hmm, but `data.clone()` requires `Data` to be `Clone`, which isn't guaranteed. 
Also, counting elements by cloning wastes effort. Let me think about this 
differently.



The issue is that `munge` takes `data: Data` by value, and 
`IntoIterator::into_iter` consumes it. We can't count the elements without 
consuming the iterator.



The stateful position needs to advance by the number of elements processed. But 
since we're returning a lazy iterator, we don't know how many elements will be 
processed until iteration completes.



One approach: make the returned iterator itself track and update the position. 
